{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08, stable label 2.08-stable; also the current release). All three groups are classified as separate families inside a single superfamily: Ferritin-like superfamily a.25.1 (sunid 47240), within the Ferritin-like fold a.25 (sunid 47239), class a (All alpha proteins, sunid 46456). The families are: Ferritin a.25.1.1 (sunid 47241); Ribonucleotide reductase-like a.25.1.2 (sunid 47253); Manganese catalase (T-catalase) a.25.1.3 (sunid 100951, with an automatic Pfam mapping to PF05067). Lineages for all three were retrieved in the same release and share class, fold, and superfamily; they diverge only at the family level. The superfamily lists ten families in total (a.25.1.1 through a.25.1.9 plus a.25.1.0 automated matches), so these three are part of a wider homologous group. The fold a.25 also contains five other superfamilies (a.25.2 Cobalamin adenosyltransferase-like, a.25.3 EsxAB dimer-like, a.25.4 PE/PPE dimer-like, a.25.5 HP0062-like, a.25.6 SO2669-like). Curator comments: the fold is described as 'core: 4 helices; bundle, closed, left-handed twist; 1 crossover connection'; the superfamily comment is 'contains bimetal-ion centre in the middle of the bundle'. The homology endpoint reports homology_implied_by_level true with scope 'sf' for a.25.1 and scope 'fa' for each of the three families, with no warnings or heterogeneity flags on the fold, superfamily, or the three families. The fold node carries the standard warning that levels above superfamily do not imply homology.

Interpretation at the correct evolutionary strength. (1) Superfamily level: SCOPe asserts probable common ancestry among ferritins, ribonucleotide-reductase-like proteins, and manganese catalases. The basis recorded by SCOPe is structural: a shared four-helix bundle core with the characteristic crossover connection, plus a conserved bimetal-ion centre located inside the bundle. This is the deepest homology-bearing level the three share. (2) Family level: SCOPe places them in different families, meaning it does not claim the closer relationship (recognisable sequence similarity or very similar function and structure) between them. Their chemistries differ: iron storage and mineralization through a ferroxidase centre in ferritins; radical generation and diverse oxygenase chemistry in the RNR-like family; hydrogen peroxide disproportionation at a dimanganese site in Mn catalases. Within SCOPe, that divergence is expressed as family separation under a common superfamily, i.e. divergent evolution of chemistry on a conserved di-metal scaffold. (3) Note that the RNR-like family a.25.1.2 itself is chemically heterogeneous: its protein entries include ribonucleotide reductase R2, methane monooxygenase hydroxylase alpha and beta subunits, toluene/o-xylene monooxygenase subunits TouA and TouE, stearoyl-ACP desaturases, and the phenylacetic acid degradation protein PaaC. So even a shared family in this superfamily spans reductase, monooxygenase, and desaturase reactions; family membership here signals a conserved di-iron carboxylate-bridged scaffold, not one reaction. (4) Fold level: the other five superfamilies in fold a.25 share only the helical topology. SCOPe makes no homology assertion for them relative to a.25.1, and that absence must not be upgraded to 'convergent' or 'unrelated'.

Limits. SCOPe superfamily membership supports common ancestry but does not license function transfer: one cannot infer substrate, metal identity (iron versus manganese), oxidation state, reaction type, oligomeric assembly (e.g. 24-mer ferritin cages versus hexameric Mn catalases), or active-site residue roles from a.25.1 placement alone. Using a ferritin structure as a template for a Mn catalase, or an RNR R2 structure for a ferritin, is appropriate only for the shared bundle core; active-site geometry, metal ligands, loops, and quaternary contacts require family-level or protein-level evidence. The API responses expose the classification and the curator comments but not the alignments, literature, or reasoning behind the superfamily grouping; the structural/mechanistic rationale beyond the recorded comments was not verified here. Confirming functional or mechanistic claims about any of the three groups requires external evidence: active-site and metal-ligand comparison from inspected structures, sequence alignment of core positions, biochemical assays, and the primary literature. Representative selection for any of the families was not performed; the ASTRAL quality endpoint would be needed for that, and the automated-matches nodes (a.25.1.0 and the automated protein entries under each family) should be reported as automated when used.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ferritin&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=manganese%20catalase&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ribonucleotide%20reductase&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47239/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1.1","/api/v1/r